  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The velocity $v$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of a particle at time $t$ (in seconds) is given by $v(t)=3{t}^{2}-24t+45.$ Calculate the velocity of the particle when the acceleration $a$ is equal to $0\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-24t+45) \\\\  & =6t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=0,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =6t-24 \\\\ 6t & =24 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, to calculate the velocity at time $t=4,$ we substitute this into the equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(4) & =3(4)^{2}-24(4)+45 \\\\  & =48-96+45 \\\\  & =-3\\text{\u00a0m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity when $a=0\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $-3\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
